# Build context exclusions for all five Dockerfiles.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY ONE DOCKER READS. A `.dockerignore` is read from the build
# CONTEXT ROOT, and `docker-publish.yml` builds every image with `context: .`.
# The only per-Dockerfile form BuildKit honours is `<dockerfile>.dockerignore`
# — `apps/backend/api/Dockerfile.dockerignore`, not `apps/backend/api/
# .dockerignore`. Three files of the latter name existed until SC-650, each
# carrying a correct `**/.env` rule that Docker never read: measured against
# the real `apps/backend/api/Dockerfile`, `/app/apps/backend/api/.env` was in
# the build stage regardless. They were deleted rather than renamed, because
# renaming them would change what three production images build from and that
# is a separate decision (SC-655).
#
# `node_modules` is the one that matters. Each Dockerfile's deps stage runs its
# own filtered `bun install --linker=hoisted` into `/app/node_modules`, and the
# build stage then does `COPY packages ./packages`. Bun's default linker leaves
# a `node_modules` directory inside every workspace holding symlinks up into the
# repo root — so on any machine that has run `bun install`, that COPY drops
# dangling symlinks into the image which shadow the hoisted tree, and the build
# fails with `File not found "/app/packages/infra/email/node_modules/zod"`.
#
# This never surfaced before because the deploy workflow builds with
# `flyctl deploy --remote-only` from a checkout that never had `bun install` run
# on it. The CI accessibility gate boots the same stack on a runner that *has*,
# which is how it was found (V3-17).
#
# Each rule is written twice because a bare `node_modules` matches only the
# context root — Docker matches patterns against the whole relative path.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. Regenerated inside the image; copying the host's only invites a
# stale binary, or one built for the wrong platform.
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
coverage
**/coverage

# Never belongs in an image. `.env` in particular is mounted at runtime by
# compose rather than baked in.
#
# The `**/` twin is load-bearing here and its absence was a real leak (SC-650).
# `scripts/sync-env.ts` writes a per-app `.env`, mode 600, into every app
# directory; a bare `.env` matches the context root only, so all seven reached
# the build context of every image. Measured against the real
# `apps/backend/api/Dockerfile`: `/app/apps/backend/api/.env` was present in the
# build stage while the root `.env` was correctly absent — the control that
# makes the gap readable rather than looking like a broken ignore file.
#
# The negations come last: `**/.env.*` matches `.env.example` too, and Docker
# applies the last matching rule.
.git
**/.git
.env
**/.env
.env.*
**/.env.*
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Editor, tool and OS droppings. Nothing in an image reads them, and a log can
# carry anything the process it came from was holding. Carried over from the
# three per-app `.dockerignore` files deleted in SC-650, which listed them and
# were never read by Docker — see the note at the top of this file.
**/.turbo
**/.cache
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store

# Test and capture artefacts — large, and nothing in an image reads them.
apps/e2e/shots
apps/e2e/test-results
apps/e2e/playwright-report
